WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.277
Function transformations change inputs or outputs in controlled ways

00:00:04.277 --> 00:00:09.015
Start with the squaring graph, whose vertex is at the origin

00:00:09.015 --> 00:00:17.200
Keeping track of a recognizable point, such as this vertex, makes each later change easier to explain and verify.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:23.742
Replacing the input by x minus two shifts the graph right by two

00:00:23.742 --> 00:00:29.791
To deliver the old input zero into the rule, the new x must be two

00:00:29.791 --> 00:00:35.033
This input-solving explanation is more reliable than memorizing that inside signs look reversed.

00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:40.515
Multiplying outside the function doubles its outputs while leaving inputs fixed

00:00:40.515 --> 00:00:48.499
The vertex remains at two, zero, but a point one unit from the vertex now has height two instead of one

00:00:48.499 --> 00:00:50.400
This is a vertical stretch.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:56.534
Subtracting three after the function evaluation shifts every output down three

00:00:56.534 --> 00:01:03.566
The vertex becomes two, negative three, and the point at input three drops to negative one

00:01:03.566 --> 00:01:08.400
Read the operations in their actual order to combine transformations correctly.
